.TH ASKTIME 1
.SH NAME
asktime \- ask for the date and time at boot
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B asktime
.RB [ \-t
.IR seconds ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The rosco_m68k has no real-time clock.
.B /etc/rc
sets the clock to the time the boot image was made
.RB ( /etc/stamp ),
then runs
.B asktime
on the console to ask for the real time.
.B Asktime
shows the time the clock is at and reads the date and time as
.RI [[ MMDDYY ] hhmm [ ss ]]
in GMT, the format
.BR date (1)
takes.  A valid answer is set with
.BR /bin/date .
RETURN, an answer that is not in this format, or no answer within the
timeout keeps the clock as it is.  On a timeout the line typed so far is
thrown away.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-t " seconds"
The timeout, 30 seconds if not given.
.SH FILES
.TP 15n
.B /etc/rc
Runs
.B "asktime \-t 30"
on /dev/console.
.TP
.B /etc/stamp
The time the boot image was made.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR date (1),
.BR cron (8).
